Humans deliberate — we weigh reasons, imagine alternatives, consult our values, and then act. This process is qualitatively different from a billiard ball rolling down an incline.
Neurological studies show that the prefrontal cortex is uniquely enlarged in humans compared to other primates, providing the biological substrate for the kind of reflective, reason-responsive deliberation associated with free will.
Compatibilist philosophers (Hume, Frankfurt, Fischer) argue that freedom is not about escaping causation but about whether your actions flow from your own desires and reasoning — and humans clearly have this.
The experience of deliberation is phenomenologically real: we do not merely observe ourselves deciding, we undergo the process of weighing options, and that experience shapes what we actually do.
